{"allowed_modes":["walk","fly","instantly"],"category":"Architectural style","citations":[{"archived_url":"","citation":"Glessner, John J. The Story of a House. Unpublished typescript booklet written for his children George and Frances, signed 'Faithfully your father, John J. Glessner', March 1923. Transcribed in full in HABS No. ILL-1015, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, data pages 7-21 (1963).","source_id":"glessner_story_of_a_house_1923","tier":2,"tier_label":"near-primary recollection","url":"","what_it_does_not_supply":["any dimension","the extent of the ivy or creeper in any year","when the stable became a garage"],"what_it_supplies":["the roof covering as built: red baked tiles, unglazed","the street walls of Wellesley granite; all walls lined with hollow brick","the courtyard elevation of common brick of slightly pinkish colour with grey limestone trimmings, matched in the adjoining house's wall","the courtyard entrance from the alley, used on moving day in December 1887","Virginia creeper on the courtyard walls (carried from the old house, 1887) and Boston ivy on the street front (from plants sent after June 1887)","the service entrance 'through the great arch on Eighteenth Street'; the Eighteenth Street door and back stairs","outside iron-work Bower-Barffed rather than painted"]},{"archived_url":"","citation":"'A Heritage Preserved', Prairie Avenue Historic District interpretive marker, Chicago; transcription on the Historical Marker Database (HMdb), marker 308723.","source_id":"hmdb_prairie_heritage_preserved","tier":4,"tier_label":"later scholarship","url":"https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=308723","what_it_does_not_supply":["anything about the houses' state in 1904 beyond what each entry dates"],"what_it_supplies":["year built, architect and style as the marker states them for 1800, 1801, 1811, 1900 and 1919 Prairie"]},{"archived_url":"","citation":"\"Residence of Miner T. Ames, Chicago. Cobb & Frost, Architects.\" Photographic plate. The Inland Architect and News Record, vol. XII, no. 3 (October 1888), plate section ('Our Illustrations').","source_id":"inland_architect_1888_10_ames_residence","tier":1,"tier_label":"period/eyewitness","url":"https://artic.contentdm.oclc.org/digital/collection/mqc/id/4518","what_it_does_not_supply":["the north flank, the rear and the plan behind the front: the 1911 Sanborn sheet 28 supplies the plan","dimensions: no scale is printed; lengths come from the 1911 sheet","colour: a monochrome print"],"what_it_supplies":["the street front's composition: the north bay, the entrance block, the oriel, their storey lines and openings, read at the scale the 1911 sheet's width gives the front","the south flank's storeys and that it is brick","the roof forms seen from the street: the gable over the bay, the pyramid over the oriel, the main roof behind, two stacks"]}],"content_version":1,"default_mode":"walk","endings":[{"completion_eligible":true,"id":"finish","read_s":10,"text":"End of the Romanesque walk. The style's marks are rough stone, round arches and towers; you can find them on other fronts along the avenue."}],"evidence":[{"confidence":"attested","id":"ames-marker","locator":"The 1811 S. Prairie Avenue entry.","sources":["hmdb_prairie_heritage_preserved"],"text":"Joseph G. Coleman Residence. Built 1886; Cobb & Frost, architects. Cobb & Frost were well known for their designs in the Romanesque style, executed here in rough-faced brown sandstone."},{"confidence":"attested","id":"ames-plate","locator":"The whole plate: the street front and south flank from the roadway south-west of the house.","sources":["inland_architect_1888_10_ames_residence"],"text":"The 1888 plate shows a rock-faced brown-stone front with a deep round arch on clustered columns over a broad stair, and a corbelled oriel over the arch."},{"confidence":"attested","id":"glessner-fabric","locator":"HABS transcription, data pages 13-15; construction description, not the 1923 garage conversion.","sources":["glessner_story_of_a_house_1923"],"text":"Glessner describes unglazed red roof tiles and granite street walls as part of the house's construction."},{"confidence":"attested","id":"glessner-marker","locator":"The 1800 S. Prairie Avenue entry.","sources":["hmdb_prairie_heritage_preserved"],"text":"John Glessner Residence. Built 1887; Henry Hobson Richardson, architect. Designed in the Richardsonian Romanesque style, the house is wrapped around a spacious light-filled courtyard."},{"confidence":"reconstructed","id":"r2011-draft","liberty":"L-draft-prairie-20-22-east-2352","sources":[],"text":"The district draft reads 2011 Prairie from the T-1837 register as Romanesque: a rough ashlar front, a round-arched entrance and a rounded corner turret under a conical cap."},{"confidence":"reconstructed","id":"rees-draft","liberty":"L-draft-prairie-20-22-west-2351","sources":[],"text":"The district draft reads the Harriet Rees house (Cobb & Frost, 1888) from the T-1837 register as Romanesque: a limestone front under a tall gable facing the avenue; its carved tympanum, loggia and bowed oriel are later refinements."},{"confidence":"reconstructed","id":"tour","liberty":"L-jaunt-prairie-walks","sources":[],"text":"The route, its order, its reading times, its keepsake and its framing are modern interpretation; no historical visit, conversation or occupant on 1 July 1904 is asserted."}],"featured":false,"id":"prairie-style-romanesque","keepsake":{"family":"News & Knowledge","id":"prairie-romanesque-note","text":"A modern field note on the style's marks, not a period document.","title":"Romanesque field note"},"liberties":["L-draft-prairie-20-22-east-2352","L-draft-prairie-20-22-west-2351","L-jaunt-prairie-walks"],"opening":{"read_s":13,"text":"STYLE WALK: ROMANESQUE. Look for heavy rough-faced stone, deep round arches and turrets. Four houses, 1800 to 2110 Prairie, on foot. Two are still first rough models, read from the district register."},"places":{"armour_corwith_house_1945_prairie":"Armour-Cor